      "id": "AC-027",
      "criterion": "Given a .tsx file containing a component declaration recognized by any of AC-001 through AC-007, AC-024, or AC-025 under some name `X` (present in SourceData.component_mappings at the point the whole file has been walked), and a static-member assignment of the exact form `X.displayName = \"NewName\"` where the right-hand side is a SINGLE STRING LITERAL (single- or double-quoted; a template literal \u2014 e.g. `` X.displayName = `NewName`; `` \u2014 does NOT match, even one with no interpolated expressions, and the assignment is left with no effect on X's entry) appearing anywhere in the file \u2014 its lexical position relative to X's own declaration does not matter, since this rule is applied only after the whole file has been walked, not at the point either statement is visited \u2014 the SourceData ultimately returned by parse_file() contains no entry keyed by `X`; that entry is instead present keyed by `NewName`, carrying the same props type name `X`'s entry carried before the reassignment was applied. If two or more distinct `displayName` reassignments target the same original name `X` in the same file, only the FIRST (lexically earliest in file-scan order) reassignment's target name is present in the final SourceData \u2014 the first rename changes the mapping's key away from `X`, so a second, later reassignment's own lookup for `X` finds no matching entry and has no effect; this is a consequence of the rename being applied via a single first-match lookup per pending rename, in file-scan order, not a distinct rule requiring separate verification. A `displayName` assignment whose right-hand side is not a string literal (e.g. a variable reference or a template literal) produces no rename and no diagnostic. A `displayName` assignment whose left-hand object name does not match any name currently present in SourceData.component_mappings at rename time produces no rename, no diagnostic, and no error.",
