  "id": "SPEC-SERIALIZATION-001",
  "requirement_id": "REQ-SERIALIZATION-001",
  "purpose": "Guarantee that every consumer-facing projection of ExtractionOutput -- RDT-compat JSON, Storybook JSON, TOON text, and the exported JSON Schema -- faithfully represents the canonical data, with no silent truncation, no misleading enum-shapes, and no undetected schema drift, so tools that treat oxc-react-docgen as a drop-in react-docgen-typescript replacement can trust the output without re-deriving it from the resolver's internal types.",
  "scope": [
    "crates/cli/src/commands/extract.rs: serialize_rdt, rdt_type_json (serialize_storybook is referenced only in non_goals for context -- no acceptance criterion here constrains its output shape)",
    "crates/core/src/toon.rs: render_output_toon, render_component_toon, format_type_compact, truncate_with_indicator",
    "crates/cli/src/commands/schema.rs: schema_value, cmd_schema, and its drift-detection test",
    "crates/core/src/types/output.rs: PropType::is_literal_union() as the single gate rdt_type_json must route through",
    "crates/core/src/resolver/mod.rs: ResolvedChain::give_up / empty_with_compose (mod.rs:376-390) -- note this is a distinct function from OpaqueDetail::give_up in types/output.rs, which returns PropType::Opaque and never touches composes; only ResolvedChain::give_up's output is in scope here, insofar as it must round-trip through serialize_rdt"
  ],
  "non_goals": [
    "The schema-coverage test (schema_covers_every_field_name_the_real_output_serializes) is a targeted substring-presence check over 5 sampled nesting points (component, one prop, one inheritance layer, one diagnostic, stats) in one hand-built fixture. It does NOT verify nesting/placement correctness, does NOT verify declared JSON types match real serialized types, does NOT walk nested substructures outside those 5 paths (e.g. defaultValue/tags/declarations internals), and does NOT catch a schema field that's stale/removed on the Rust side. This spec does not attempt to close any of those gaps -- deriving schemars::JsonSchema (ADR-0006, proposed not accepted) is out of scope here.",
    "The underlying PropType/CollectedType representation the resolver produces is out of scope -- this spec covers only how an already-resolved ComponentEntry tree is serialized.",
    "Mapped types, conditional types, and other real-type-inference gaps OXC does not attempt are tracked in docs/type-checker-integration.md and rdt-coverage.md, not re-litigated here.",
    "ref/key omission from all three formats is a locked, separately-documented decision (Invariant 5 in the serialization-formats semantic model) -- not reopened by this spec.",
    "methods: [] being hardcoded empty in both RDT and Storybook output is a locked design decision (class components out of scope) -- not reopened by this spec.",
    "Distinguishing, within the composes field itself, RDT's native 'props from elsewhere' population from this resolver's 'gave up resolving' population is explicitly not required -- Invariant 4 documents this as an intentional, undifferentiated reuse; a consumer must cross-reference diagnostics to disambiguate, and that mechanism is not part of this spec.",
    "Whether serialize_storybook should or should not include an enum-shaped type, a composes key, or a parent key is not asserted by this spec in either direction. The requirement's 'faithfully representing the underlying data without misrepresenting it' language does not clearly mandate Storybook's current field omissions (Invariant 6 in the serialization-formats semantic model) over the alternative of including them, so no acceptance criterion locks in or changes serialize_storybook's current shape -- a future spec should address Storybook format fidelity explicitly if that becomes a stakeholder concern.",
    "SPEC-CLI-001a's AC-016 currently asserts the same schema_covers_every_field_name_the_real_output_serializes test property this spec's AC-6/AC-7 assert, including its 'ExtractionStats whose every numeric field is non-zero' clause; AC-7 below now fully subsumes that clause (stats assertions included) so the two specs no longer conflict, but they still duplicate the same test's contract. This spec claims sole ownership going forward; SPEC-CLI-001a should be updated to defer to this spec rather than re-specifying it, but that edit is not made by this spec (cross-spec edits are out of scope for a single spec@1 document -- flagged for the spec owner/reviewer to reconcile)."
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-1",
      "description": "rdt_type_json(&PropType::Union(vec![])) equals exactly json!({\"name\": \"\"}); rdt_type_json(&PropType::Union(vec![PropType::StringLiteral(\"x\".into())])) equals exactly json!({\"name\": \"\\\"x\\\"\"}); rdt_type_json(&PropType::LiteralUnion { members: vec![], has_default: false }) equals exactly json!({\"name\": \"\"}); rdt_type_json(&PropType::LiteralUnion { members: vec![\"only\".into()], has_default: false }) equals exactly json!({\"name\": \"\\\"only\\\"\"}) -- all four verified with assert_eq! against the exact json!() literal shown (not a partial/contains or name-is-not-\"enum\" check), so a regression that produces the wrong non-enum shape, not just an enum shape, is caught too.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-2",
      "description": "rdt_type_json(&PropType::Union(vec![PropType::StringLiteral(\"red\".into()), PropType::NumberLiteral(5.0)])) equals exactly json!({\"name\": \"enum\", \"value\": [{\"value\": \"\\\"red\\\"\"}, {\"value\": \"5\"}]}) -- the StringLiteral member's \"value\" is the JSON string containing two literal double-quote characters around red, the NumberLiteral member's \"value\" is the bare digit string with no quotes added. Separately, rdt_type_json(&PropType::LiteralUnion { members: vec![\"red\".into(), \"blue\".into()], has_default: false }) equals exactly json!({\"name\": \"enum\", \"value\": [{\"value\": \"\\\"red\\\"\"}, {\"value\": \"\\\"blue\\\"\"}]}) -- every LiteralUnion member's \"value\" is quote-wrapped unconditionally, regardless of content. Both fixtures are verified with assert_eq! against the exact json!() literal shown, not a partial/contains check.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-3",
      "description": "For a fixture table spanning PropType::Union and PropType::LiteralUnion at 0, 1, 2, and 3 members, plus a PropType::Union at 2 members with one non-literal (e.g. Named) member mixed in, rdt_type_json returns the {\"name\": \"enum\", ...} shape if and only if prop_type.is_literal_union() returns true for that same value -- verified by calling both functions on each fixture value and asserting the equivalence directly (not by inspecting rdt_type_json's source).",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-4",
      "description": "For a PropType::Union with 2+ members where at least one member is not a StringLiteral/NumberLiteral/BoolLiteral (e.g. one Named type mixed with one StringLiteral), rdt_type_json returns {\"name\": <raw_string>} and never the enum shape -- verified with a 3-member fixture containing exactly one non-literal member.",
