This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   The overarching task, which evolved through multiple explicit user directives across this long session, was to conduct and then act on a rigorous "spec-drift" quality audit of a prior SDD (spec-driven-development) initiative in the `oxc-react-docgen` Rust codebase, and then continue closing every gap found until the codebase's test coverage and correctness were exhaustively solid. In order, the explicit user requests were:
   1. Read `docs/superpowers/session-handoff-2026-08-12.md` for context, then run `canon-drift-checker` against each of the 7 gated `spec@1` documents in `.claude/specs/`, specifically scrutinizing whether newly-added tests actually assert what their spec criteria require (not just that they pass). **"Report findings before fixing anything — I want to review what it finds before you touch code."**
   2. When subagent delegation stalled overnight with no results: **"You are losing track of the work somehow, check on it because it ran overnight so we should have had results. WTF"** — demanding I actively investigate rather than passively wait.
   3. **"Don't wait for the rest, run those checks yourself anyway"** — do the remaining drift-checks manually instead of continuing to rely on unreliable subagent delegation.
   4. **"Do BINDING, EXTRACTOR, and SERIALIZATION with the same rigor"** — apply the same deep, full-spec-criterion scrutiny to specs I'd under-scoped in my first manual pass.
   5. **"Start with the AC-017 severe finding first"** then (mid-turn) **"Then process the other findings"** — fix the most severe finding first, then work through every other finding from the entire review.
   6. **"Commit and go to the next highest priority/reward case"** — commit completed work, then autonomously identify and pursue the next-highest-value remaining item.
   7. **"Do we have all of the happy path covered by tests? Corner and error cases well covered?"** — an assessment question about overall test coverage completeness across the whole codebase, not just the reviewed specs.
   8. **"Look into it, leave no leaf untouched"** — in response to my finding that `react_types.rs` had thin test coverage, exhaustively test every code path/match-arm in that module.
   9. **"Continue with all the open items"** — after I listed remaining known gaps (P0-2 cache staleness, `resolve_named` cycle detection, `component_cache` staleness, 8 component-shape detection gaps, PTY-limited paths), continue closing them.
   10. Via `AskUserQuestion`, when I flagged that P0-2 (deliberately deferred by a past session) and the 8 component-shape gaps (new feature work, not bug fixes) needed an explicit decision before I touched them: user chose **"Fix it now"** for P0-2, and **"Implement all 8"** for the component-shape detection gaps.

   Throughout, the user's implicit standard (established via their corrections and my own adopted discipline) was: every fix must be verified via genuine fail-then-pass testing (deliberately break the fix, confirm the test catches it, restore the fix), `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings` and `cargo fmt --all -- --check` must be clean after each change, the full `cargo test --workspace` suite must pass, `docs/STATUS.md`'s test count must be kept current, and `docs/edge-cases.md`/`docs/root-cause-analysis.md` must be updated to reflect fixes (strikethrough + **FIXED**, preserving audit trail per that doc's own stated convention). Commits should be made per logical unit of work, with no line wrapping, succinct and engineering-focused, ending with the `Claude-Session:` footer and explicitly NOT a `Co-Authored-By` line (per the user's persistent global memory preference).

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Rust workspace with 3 crates: `crates/core` (pure extraction/resolution logic, no I/O/terminal code), `crates/cli` (clap-based CLI, delegates to core), `crates/binding` (NAPI wrapper for Node.js/Vite).
   - SDD (spec-driven-development) pipeline: `canon-drafter` → `canon-verifier` → `canon-auditor` → `canon-exit-gate` for spec authoring; `canon-drift-checker` for post-implementation re-verification (classifies each acceptance criterion as covered/uncovered/drifted).
   - 7 gated `spec@1` documents in `.claude/specs/`: `SPEC-PIPELINE-001`, `SPEC-EXTRACTOR-001`, `SPEC-RESOLVER-001`, `SPEC-TYPES-001`, `SPEC-SERIALIZATION-001`, `SPEC-BINDING-001`, and `SPEC-CLI-001a/b/c/d` (4 sub-specs for exit codes, atomic writes, LSP framing, config loading).
   - "Assert content, not presence" testing discipline (project CLAUDE.md non-negotiable): prefer `assert_eq!` against exact expected values over `contains()`/`is_some()` liveness checks.
   - "Sibling-path parity" testing discipline: a function mirroring an already-tested one needs its own equivalent test.
   - Fail-then-pass verification: for every fix, temporarily revert/disable it, confirm the new test fails, then restore and confirm it passes — used extensively and consistently throughout.
   - `ResolveState` (in `crates/core/src/types/global.rs`): shared mutable state threaded through resolver functions, containing `visited` (chain.rs's permanent cycle-detection set, cloned per-branch to avoid diamond-inheritance false positives), the newly-added `named_in_progress` (resolve_named's own DFS-stack cycle-detection set, insert-on-entry/remove-on-exit), `diagnostics`, `in_scope_type_params`.
   - Diamond-inheritance false-positive bug class: sharing one permanent "visited" set across sibling branches or independent call sites incorrectly flags legitimate repeated/shared references as cycles. This exact bug class was found and fixed TWICE this session (once in `chain.rs`'s `resolve_interface_chain`, once avoided-by-design in the NEW `resolve_named` fix).
   - `rustc_hash::FxHasher` — fast, non-cryptographic hasher, already a dependency (via `rustc-hash` crate), used both for `FxHashMap`/`FxHashSet` throughout the codebase and now directly for `cache.rs`'s content-hashing.
   - `write_atomic` pattern (temp-file-then-rename) for crash-safe file writes, shared between `extract --out` and `watch --out` via `crates/cli/src/output.rs`.
   - `DashMap` (from the `dashmap` crate) used for `WatchSession::component_cache`, `DtsCache::store`.
   - `rstest` crate's `#[rstest]` + `#[case(...)]` parameterized test macro — IMPORTANT GOTCHA: `grep -c "#\[test\]"` does NOT count `#[rstest]`-annotated tests, since rstest generates the actual test functions via macro expansion. I made this exact mistake once (undercounted `react_types.rs`'s existing coverage) and had to self-correct.
   - `Arc::ptr_eq` — used to prove object IDENTITY (same underlying allocation) rather than just value equality, critical for BINDING's AC-9 "reuse not reconstruct" test.
   - MessagePack (`rmp_serde`) positional (not named-map) struct encoding for `DtsCache`'s on-disk persistence — field order/shape changes require a `CACHE_SCHEMA_VERSION` bump to invalidate old caches.
   - `ignore` crate (`WalkBuilder`) — its `git_ignore(bool)` builder method controls `.gitignore` respect; `.git/info/exclude` and `.ignore`-file support remain at crate DEFAULTS (`true`) unless explicitly overridden — this distinction was key to correctly testing `SPEC-PIPELINE-001` AC-001's gitignore matrix.
   - Pre-canonicalization vs. post-canonicalization path matching: `discover.rs`'s built-in exclusion substring checks (`.stories.`, `node_modules`, etc.) match against the WALKER's raw reported path (pre-canonicalization), while the final `file_path` stored in output is canonicalized AFTER the exclusion check — verified via a symlink-based test fixture.
   - Content-hash-based cache invalidation replacing mtime+size, eliminating same-tick/same-size false-positive cache hits at the root rather than mitigating them.

3. Files and Code Sections:

   - **`crates/core/src/pipeline/watch.rs`** (extensively modified across multiple phases)
     - AC-017 fix: rewrote `update_file_with_a_non_canonical_path_still_finds_the_canonical_entry` to add a `Consumer.tsx` file that imports from the symlinked `Button.tsx`, proving the transitive-dependent case (since `affected()` trivially includes the changed file itself via BFS self-seeding, the original test couldn't distinguish canonicalized from non-canonicalized behavior).
     - Added 4 new tests: `snapshot_and_update_file_on_an_uninitialized_session_do_not_panic` (AC-020), `update_file_after_an_atomic_replace_reflects_the_new_content_not_a_deletion` (AC-015), `deleting_a_file_surfaces_io_error_and_a_dependent_stops_resolving_against_it` (AC-019), `update_file_with_non_utf8_content_surfaces_io_error_and_skips_that_files_own_component` (AC-024).
     - Strengthened `session_recovers_after_a_contained_panic_and_serves_unrelated_files_normally` to check prop content (`x.props.contains_key("y")`) not just presence.
     - `component_cache` staleness fix — key code:
     ```rust
     // A failed read (deletion, permission error, non-UTF8 content) must
     // NOT evict this file's cache entries — SPEC-PIPELINE-001's AC-019
     // deliberately specifies that a deleted file's previously-resolved
     // component stays in component_cache/snapshot() until a new
     // WatchSession is constructed...
     let file_read_failed = io_diagnostic.is_some();

     let mut updated_components = Vec::new();
     let mut diagnostics: Vec<Diagnostic> = io_diagnostic.into_iter().collect();

     if !file_read_failed {
         let stale_keys: Vec<String> = self
             .component_cache
             .iter()
             .filter(|r| r.value().file_path == changed)
             .map(|r| r.key().clone())
             .collect();
         for key in stale_keys {
             self.component_cache.remove(&key);
         }
     }

     for (entry, diags) in results {
         let key = if self.component_cache.iter().any(|r| r.value().display_name == entry.display_name) {
             format!("{} ({})", entry.display_name, entry.file_path)
         } else {
             entry.display_name.clone()
         };
         self.component_cache.insert(key, entry.clone());
         updated_components.push(entry);
         diagnostics.extend(diags);
     }
     ```
     - Added tests `renaming_a_component_between_edits_evicts_its_old_cache_key` and `editing_a_disambiguated_component_updates_its_own_key_not_the_bare_one` (the latter proved actual cross-file data corruption in the old code; needed a `.canonicalize_utf8()` fix for macOS `/var` vs `/private/var` symlink mismatch in the test itself).

   - **`crates/core/src/pipeline/mod.rs`**
     - AC-010 (3rd-occurrence-wins) test strengthened with exact prop-content and disambiguated-key checks.
     - Added ~6 new tests for `SPEC-PIPELINE-001` AC-001's gitignore/`.ignore`/`.git` work-tree matrix: `gitignore_excludes_a_matching_file_inside_a_real_git_work_tree`, `gitignore_has_no_effect_when_the_src_dir_string_contains_node_modules`, `ignore_file_excludes_a_matching_file_with_no_exception`, `gitignore_has_no_effect_outside_any_git_work_tree_but_ignore_still_does`, `a_src_dir_physically_under_node_modules_but_not_named_that_way_is_not_exempt` (uses a real symlink to prove both the negative-pair non-exemption AND the pre-canonicalization substring-matching claim in one fixture).
     - For P0-2: updated `merge_cached_dts_file` to read content once and pass to both `cache.get`/`cache.insert`; updated the Phase 2 parallel parse loop to read the file BEFORE checking cache (previously checked cache first via stat-only, now must read for hashing) while preserving `io_diag` semantics correctly on both hit/miss paths.

   - **`crates/core/src/resolver/chain.rs`**
     - Diamond-inheritance fix: `resolve_interface_chain`'s extends loop now clones `state.visited` per branch:
     ```rust
     for extends_ref in &iface.extends {
         let mut branch_state = ResolveState {
             visited: state.visited.clone(),
             named_in_progress: state.named_in_progress.clone(),
             diagnostics: vec![],
             in_scope_type_params: state.in_scope_type_params.clone(),
         };
         let (parent_chain, maybe_layer) =
             resolve_extends_ref(extends_ref, &iface.file_path, mapping, ctx, &mut branch_state, depth + 1);
         state.diagnostics.extend(branch_state.diagnostics);
         ...
     }
     ```

   - **`crates/core/src/resolver/named.rs`**
     - `resolve_named` rewritten as a cycle-detecting wrapper delegating to a new private `resolve_named_uncycled`:
     ```rust
     pub(super) fn resolve_named(
         name: &CompactString, args: &[CollectedType], consuming_file: &Utf8Path,
         ctx: &ResolutionContext, state: &mut ResolveState, depth: u8,
     ) -> PropType {
         let visit_key: CompactString = format!(
             "{}:{}<{}>", consuming_file, name,
             args.iter().map(|a| a.to_raw_string()).collect::<Vec<_>>().join(",")
         ).into();

         if !state.named_in_progress.insert(visit_key.clone()) {
             state.diagnostics.push(Diagnostic {
                 severity: DiagnosticSeverity::Info,
                 message: format!("Circular type reference detected resolving '{}' in '{}' — stopping here to avoid infinite recursion", name, consuming_file),
                 ...
                 code: DiagnosticCode::MaxDepthExceeded,
             });
             return OpaqueDetail::new(name.to_string(), OpaqueReason::DepthExceeded);
         }

         let result = resolve_named_uncycled(name, args, consuming_file, ctx, state, depth);
         state.named_in_progress.remove(&visit_key);
         result
     }
     ```

   - **`crates/core/src/resolver/alias.rs`**
     - Two `branch_state` construction sites updated to include `named_in_progress: state.named_in_progress.clone()`.

   - **`crates/core/src/types/global.rs`**
     - Added `named_in_progress: FxHashSet<CompactString>` field to `ResolveState`, with an extensive doc comment explaining why it's separate from `visited` (avoiding the false-positive class).

   - **`crates/core/src/resolver/mod.rs`** — extensively edited across multiple phases:
     - Strengthened AC-2 test (exact severity/code/message + composes check), AC-2b test (added composes assertion), AC-3/AC-4/AC-6 exact-message tests, diamond-inheritance regression test `diamond_inheritance_does_not_falsely_report_a_cycle`.
     - Added `self_referential_type_alias_used_directly_as_a_field_type_is_caught_immediately` and `two_unrelated_fields_sharing_the_same_type_are_not_falsely_flagged_as_a_cycle` for the resolve_named fix.

